patch: relop-long-fold
file: n1/out.c 516d8b7c37baf86c44cc8c0c83f3a5471095f5f3b843ac9afbb7ef84fe0a4ca4
file: n1/sel0.c 640bdfe6742a65726da0ae384eb1c52a02b8e401bfe91d7b011921fa80ccef53
fix: `!islong(...)` -- a long compare-vs-0 must not fold to a direct operand test
why: Z8001: no test-long reaches the relop selector; the fold drops the explicit `CPL n,#0`. (Z8001-target edit: this tree builds the Z8001 compiler only. The donor i386 coder needs the fold; the i386 host harness uses pristine donor source.)

patch: subgoal-reg-leaf
file: n1/out.c 516d8b7c37baf86c44cc8c0c83f3a5471095f5f3b843ac9afbb7ef84fe0a4ca4
fix: subgoal-output sites skip re-materializing an operand already a `REG` leaf
why: Z8001 far pointers arrive in a register pair as a REG leaf; re-routing one through `outtree` derefs a garbage `t_lp` (FUZZ-13 SIGSEGV)

patch: opeq0-convert-narrow
file: n1/mtree0.c 0841ad1b7c1785231e0154ad730a10d2bf12cc7dfdf585efde77d678351aaf38
fix: `x op= 0' folding CONVERTs a narrower lvalue to the node's promoted type instead of retyping it in place; an effect context keeps the retype
why: the value of `x op= 0' is x at the PROMOTED type, so retyping the lvalue node widens the object's own load -- on the Z8001 `char c; v = (c += 0)' emitted `ld r0,-1(r13)', a WORD load of a one-byte auto, reading the neighbouring byte as the high half (big-endian, so v became c<<8|junk). Confirmed at HEAD and fixed: the load is now `ldb rl0,-1(r13)' + `extsb', identical to plain `v = c'. Reached by AADD/ASUB/AOR/AXOR/ASHL/ASHR (AAND is rewritten to ASSIGN above); `c += 1' was never affected, only the zero fold. MEFFECT reads nothing, so it keeps the cheaper retype and no code moves

patch: subadd-keep-type
file: n1/mtree0.c 0841ad1b7c1785231e0154ad730a10d2bf12cc7dfdf585efde77d678351aaf38
fix: the SUB->ADD constant canonicalization keeps the node's own type (donor stamped `IVAL_T` "Signed")
why: the stamp writes junk signedness over the C type (`u - 1` labeled signed, `p - c` labeled non-pointer); the Z8001 MD selects signed-vs-unsigned divide/shift by node type, and the MD `fixaddtype` now preserves node signedness through reassociation (FUZZ-5), so the stamp must not lie. Donor i386 relied on the re-derive repairing it each pass

patch: cast-signedness-keep
file: n0/expr.c 941c57539fd0f9b883130b5af6c8341e6cc17bbf4fa3554d642ec38d11e1fb86
fix: LPAREN cast: don't collapse a bit-compatible cast that CHANGES signedness onto a DIV/REM/SHR/ASHR/ADIV/AREM node -- keep the CAST node
why: the collapse retypes the op in place, making `(int)(u/d) >> k` and `(int)((u/d) >> k)` the same tree with opposite correct shifts (FUZZ-20); the surviving node is the only disambiguator. Same guard the `n0/stat.c` return-coerce row already carries; cc1 selects the kept convert at zero cost (leaves.t same-kind identity)

patch: bput-eof
file: common/bput.c bbe48b328b717889f7a66a6b5b62e5b93962038e511a6f81a118f65b88c104da
fix: `putc(b, ofp) == EOF && ferror(ofp)` -- EOF alone is not failure
why: the native putc fast path returns the stored CHAR (`*_cp++ = c`), so byte 0xFF reads back as -1 == EOF; the donor's own `#if 0` comment describes this bug class but its `b &= 0xFF` guard cannot fix the macro's return value. Any IR stream containing an 0xFF byte (every negative ival) aborted "temporary file write error" on the target; the host putc returns an int and never trips it

patch: struct-member-align
file: n0/z8001/bind.c
file: h/z8001/mch.h
file: h/cc0.h cb30ca61acf7c1552bb19b91579d0d55bae0f9a77b0ea858dd371edeef3558ca
fix: word-align structure members: `fieldalign()` rounds a normal member up to its natural alignment (1 for a byte object, 2 for any wider type/pointer) via new `memalign()`; new `salign()` gives the struct itself the alignment of its widest member; `saligntype()` macro -> `salign()`; `extern int salign()` declared
why: the donor i8086 template packs members to byte offsets, which the i8086 tolerates (unaligned word/dword access); the Z8000 does NOT -- a word/long/far-pointer read at an odd address returns the wrong bytes. getty's `struct stable` put `char *s_lmsg` at odd offset 5, so `LDL 5(Rn)` loaded a garbage far pointer -> strlen SIGSEGV (the login-prompt blocker). Aligning is a hardware requirement, so the original MWC Z8001 compiler must do the same. Codegen cost: field access is still one load and array indexing still one MULT/scale -- only the displacement/size *immediate* changes, so no instruction-count change; the sole effect is a few padding bytes in mixed sub-word/word structs, which the original ABI also carries. Efficiency parity vs the original is an OPEN follow-up, to be A-B'd against the original HD-extracted binaries by disassembly (NOT a self-comparison). All difftest generators (struct/bitfield/2darr/strptr/torture) still match gcc

patch: itree-opid-buffer
file: n3/itree.c 6425d15787cc09e743a695f2f8f90756f2b7d292b53c7a1ea877b52d6394f4c2
fix: the `op >= ETCBASE` arm `sprintf`s into `opid` before `opid` points anywhere: give it a local buffer
why: writes through an uninitialized pointer. The arm fires only on an out-of-range tree opcode -- i.e. on a corrupt or foreign intermediate file, which is precisely when someone runs cc3 -- so the tool crashes exactly where it is supposed to report

patch: igen-segdir-bound
file: n3/z8001/igen.c
fix: bound the `ENTER` segment-directive lookup by `NSEGDIR`
why: `seg_enter[]`/`seg_leave[]` have an entry per LOADABLE segment (6); `ops.h` defines 11 segment codes, and `SANY` and up would index past the end. The i8086 template has the same unbounded index

patch: dspush-null
file: n0/expand.c 3ee464ba58ba524d3d5ad7b84100c18f11e211c8e50c1dcd671c4967116072df
fix: `dspush(DS_IEOF, 0)` -> `dspush(DS_IEOF, NULL)`
why: K&R: the callee takes a pointer, and a bare `0` passes an `int`. On the Z8001 that is 2 bytes into a 4-byte slot, so the argument and everything after it is misaligned. The project's most recurring bug class; harmless on the host, wrong on the target

patch: fold-operand-widen
file: n0/fold.c faa0d7434315d0d1e07f642e8d26ed091619521ea75591dd3bbe2f2a3be15d17
fix: constant folding widens each operand by ITS OWN signedness, not the goal type's
why: an unsigned operand promoted into a wider signed goal (unsigned int + long) is value-preserving and must ZERO extend; a signed operand promoted into an unsigned goal must SIGN extend. Keying both off the goal type mis-widens one or the other. The operation's own signed/unsigned semantics still use the goal `uflag`

patch: node-uival
file: n1/node.c 726975f8f958dc62ea7bef40c460b0c5de581fd38de2df07c0f2eb47c62c6675
fix: `(unsigned) tp->t_ival` -> `(uival_t) tp->t_ival`
why: `unsigned` is the HOST's width; `uival_t` is the IR's. Casting a target constant through host `unsigned` truncates it on any host wider than the target

patch: oldmachine-macro
file: n0/cc0.c 5d09169339329ac3362d31f6764ed02dd97c992d876fe9080311e2e6e10cf83b
file: h/z8001/cppmch.h
fix: new `OLDMACHINE` ("Z8001"), predefined beside `MACHINE` ("_Z8001")
why: the June 1985 compiler's standalone cpp predefines the target unprefixed, and the COHERENT 3.2 sources select on that spelling: `<l.out.h>` takes the **n.out object layout** under `#ifdef Z8001`, `sbrk()` its segment-crossing arithmetic, `exec()` its shared-library arms. A compiler that defines only `_Z8001`/`__Z8001__` builds the wrong layout silently, so this is a target-macro requirement, not a convenience. Guarded by `#ifdef`, so a machine layer that does not define it is unchanged

patch: vcpp-copy-text
file: n0/get.c cc75dea4ec472417119bd3ab6b0e8e4653a48c3db679785be9133d75caff17cd
file: n0/cpp.c e9d3190445d57ff493744babb3e6f343359b09628152e90e5b9d88845abdada7
fix: in `isvariant(VCPP)` (standalone-preprocessor) runs only: a quote literal ends at the newline instead of erroring, and a non-C byte is copied instead of erroring
why: VCPP is the mode in which cc0 is a **preprocessor**, and the text it copies is in the CONSUMER's language, not C. The assembly the kernel build preprocesses (`md.s`, `mmas.s`, `scroll.s`) has apostrophes and a stray ESC inside `/`-comments; as C those are "new line in character literal" and "illegal cpp character", and the unterminated-quote scan then ran on past the end of the line. The 1985 standalone cpp copies both without comment, and our output now matches its output on `md.s` token for token. Nothing changes on the compile path: `VCPP` is never set there

patch: stream-binary-win32
file: h/stream.h 5b2061016aeb410b92fc8f50bce5f301f2cf485db29b566c3acf87a4939f5c69
fix: new `_WIN32` arm selecting `"rb"`/`"wb"`/`"r+b"` with the pass-to-pass accessors, and the `COHERENT` arm yields to it
why: the streams cc0/cc1/cc2 pass between them are BINARY, and the host build defines `COHERENT`, whose arm asks for text mode. On Windows that turns every 0x0A written into 0x0D 0x0A and the objects are garbage, silently -- nothing about the compile fails. BAKED, not a shim: it is `#ifdef`-guarded on a platform macro exactly like the donor's own `MSDOS` and `GEMDOS` arms, which are there for the same reason, so the donor plainly anticipated the arm and no other target sees it

patch: vkern-variant
file: h/var.h bb8571f7598944b95cc906fa9fee025954fc001ad386cef67b33ae0efc656b2a
fix: new variant code `VKERN` (9)
why: the kernel model: numeric frame/auto addresses carry the system stack segment (SS = 0x3F) rather than the flat model's segment 0. Variant codes are a shared namespace, so the definition has to live with the others; nothing machine-independent reads it

patch: store-converge
file: n1/sel0.c 640bdfe6742a65726da0ae384eb1c52a02b8e401bfe91d7b011921fa80ccef53
file: n1/code.c 747566dae0a5a81d9c31afcef4616d0376bf40f107ad3340dbf05e3637affc41
file: h/cc1.h ad9eaee22669a51462c1027abede6574b6ae9b24206679793c6c4d9c2430e171
fix: `store()` records the subtree each spill was made for (`storesub[]`, beside the existing `storelist[]`), and a second demand for the same subtree is a `cbotch` naming the operator instead of a silent re-enqueue
why: `store()` spills a subtree to a stack temp and enqueues `temp = <subtree>`; if selecting that assignment cannot address the subtree either, the donor enqueues it again with the same tree and the same registers, so the list grows without converging and NSTORE decides only how long the loop runs (ICE 5149). One subtree now gets one store. This is a termination guarantee, not a codegen change: no accepted program compiles differently, and the diagnostic lands at the demand rather than as an exhausted table. The native cost is the array itself -- NSTORE is 20 off the i386 arm, so 20 near pointers of BSS in cc1, which has room; the loop it bounds is over at most 20 entries and runs once per store, so it is invisible beside selection

patch: lvalue-fixup-terminate
file: n1/sel0.c 640bdfe6742a65726da0ae384eb1c52a02b8e401bfe91d7b011921fa80ccef53
fix: `iselect()' does not fall back on an RVALUE pattern plus a fixup when the context is MLVALUE and the register is not a real one
why: a fixup leaves the value in a register of its own choosing, which is an answer to MRVALUE and not to `an lvalue in any addressing register'; the node it builds arrives back at the same test, matches the same RVALUE pattern and is fixed up again, so selection does not terminate and cc1 dies on its own stack (a far pointer dereferenced as an lvalue -- the donor's i386 can address through every register and never asks). Returning failure instead is what the caller already handles: `seltree()' goes on to its next pattern. The disabled MLVALUE-fixup arm below is the donor's own note that this machine class needs the case; nothing here re-enables it. No accepted program compiles differently -- a fixup that terminated was reached through a real register, which this leaves alone

patch: address-reg-reserve
file: n1/reg0.c 3146f8fda7a4ada5c452f42acda5131886f90742853615cdaa5bd195571ad13c
fix: `rallo()` keeps one register that can address: when an allocation would take the last register whose `r_lvalue` is non-empty, a register that can hold the kind but could never address it takes the value instead. A pointer-typed value is exempt
why: the reserve is decided entirely by the machine register table's `r_lvalue` -- the set of kinds a register can be dereferenced through -- so it is machine-independent code reading a machine-dependent fact, and on a machine where every register can address (the donor's i386) `hold` is never set and the function returns exactly what the donor returned. On the Z8001 that set is small and exhaustible: a value in the last addressable register cannot be undone, the selector then spills, and the spilled assignment wants the address it could not form -- ICE 5149, which the original 1985 compiler fails on too. Measured over 1017 sources (798 compiling), the reserve fires so rarely that exactly two objects move, awk5.o 1915 -> 1910 instructions and the kernel's bio.o 1523 -> 1521, both FEWER, and the 13 sources shared with the 1985 compiler are unchanged -- so it costs nothing against the efficiency baseline

patch: driver-z8001-model
file: coh/cc.c 0364333a706756c33c00fa901778851151d7e01e1f6926754ed37bcf1ef5346f
fix: `#if Z8001` arms in the driver's variant defaults: `VSEG` + `VREADONLY` instead of `V80186`, and the model-selection block chooses `VLARGE` unless `-VSMALL` was asked for, in place of the Intel OMF rules
why: the C900's C model is the segmented one -- a pointer is a two-word seg:offset (`h/z8001/varmch.h` VSEG) -- and the donor's default is Intel's, so a driver taking it would compile every program for a flat Z8002 with truncated pointers. `VREADONLY` goes with it because the COHERENT 3.2 headers spell const `readonly` (`ctype.h`) and without the keyword they do not parse. The Intel block below forces the small model outside OMF output; there is no OMF on this machine, and `VSEG`/`VNSEG` are one machine-dependent slot apart and must never both be set. Both arms are `#if Z8001`, so no other target sees them

patch: char-parameter-address
file: n0/ddecl.c 92064706183fdacc677581b46201d4fb56670dd2c5f2d2c6f08c301780997b5b
file: h/cc0.h cb30ca61acf7c1552bb19b91579d0d55bae0f9a77b0ea858dd371edeef3558ca
file: n0/z8001/bind.c
fix: a parameter declared `char'/`unsigned char' KEEPS that type instead of being retyped to `int': `declare()`'s C_ARG arm asks the machine layer's new `subwordparm()` first, and skips `fixtype()` when it answers yes (`extern int subwordparm()` declared in cc0.h). The machine layer then binds such a parameter at the LOW-ORDER byte of its word slot -- `bindargs()` gives it `offset+1` and still advances by 2, so every later parameter keeps its address
why: A FRONT-END EDIT MADE ON PURPOSE, owner-decided (TASK #321), because no machine layer can express the fix: after `fixtype()` retypes the parameter to `int` the fact that it was a byte object is GONE, and `&c` can only name the whole word. K&R promotes a char ARGUMENT to int at the call, so the parameter does occupy a word (unchanged: callers still push one, and the ABI is untouched) -- but the parameter is still a one-byte object, and on this BIG-ENDIAN machine the caller's byte is the second byte of that word. The donor handed out the word's own address, which is the low-order byte only because the i8086 it descends from is little-endian; here it named the HIGH byte, zero for every ASCII character, so `write(fd,&c,1)` inside `f(c) char c;` wrote a NUL -- libcurses `waddch` drew NULs while the cursor advanced, and kermit could not send a character (#324). `subwordparm()` answers from `mysizes[]`, so a machine whose char is a word gets the donor's promotion unchanged, and short/float are unaffected (a short IS a word; float is promoted to double by both sides). Reading the parameter's value now takes a byte load plus the extend the declared type asks for, where the promoted word allowed one word load; that is the price of the object having an address at all, and it is also what K&R says the object is -- `f(c) char c; c = 300;` now truncates, and a caller passing 200 into a signed `char' parameter reads -56 back, both of which every conforming compiler does (the differential oracle, gcc, agrees on all 18 generators). Code that wants the wider value must say `int' or `unsigned char', as it must anywhere else. Measured cost: over the 315 objects of this compiler's own self-host build, the ONLY two that change are the two files edited here; over 108 COHERENT userland commands the total is +76 bytes, 0.014%, in exactly the five objects that have a byte parameter -- and `units.o' gets SMALLER, because a byte compare no longer widens first. A byte parameter never reaches a register: `grabreg()` takes word types only and the promotion pass offers no parameter at all, so it stays addressable and correct. Six value assertions through cc0->cc1->cc2->ld->run cover both byte types, both directions and the second parameter's placement (tests/regress.sh, promoted from expected-fail); the byte-identity fixpoint cannot see this class at all, since a compiler reading the wrong byte agrees with itself about it

patch: function-redeclared-after-definition
file: n0/ddecl.c 92064706183fdacc677581b46201d4fb56670dd2c5f2d2c6f08c301780997b5b
fix: `declare()' restores a demoted C_GREF to the symbol's OLD class for data only. A function declared again, after it was defined earlier in the same file, keeps C_GREF and so reads as the declaration it is
why: `int f();' at file scope is demoted C_GDEF -> C_GREF a few lines above, exactly so `xdef()' will not take it for a definition. The redeclaration arm then handed the old class back, and where the old class was C_GDEF -- the function was DEFINED in this file -- `xdef()' saw a definition with no body and said "compound statement required", after which the parser was lost for the rest of the file. The declaration is legal C that every other compiler takes, and the host build takes it too, so nothing in the tree could see the difference until a unity build put a definition and a later declaration of the same function in one translation unit: src/ld/all.c, which is how the machine's own linker is compiled, and which stopped compiling. Restoring the old class is right for DATA, where `int x; ... extern int x;' must leave x defined; for a function there is nothing to preserve, since the definition has already been emitted under its own label and the class only says whether this DECLARATOR defines it. Nothing that compiled before can change: the case this reaches was a hard error, so no object in the tree was built through it -- and none is, measured: libc-z8001's 215 objects are byte-identical across the edit, and of the 490 in the compiler's own self-host build the only one that changes is `ddecl.o', whose source this is

patch: cc2-free-emitted-function
file: n2/getfun.c 33405f035e6c0ec639a0e958ca03b3bf55654e7f1afb34003d1bbf39aa68ed96
file: n2/cc2.c 3526dc610b5777794a6ebebadf8dc8e892c9fbf7e50ae11fea5ba5f10b46f24d
fix: `freefunc()' releases the instruction list of the function just emitted; cc2.c calls it after genepilog()
why: the donor never frees an INS.  getfunc() starts each function's list at
why: `ins' without looking back, so on the native C900 -- where cc2's heap is
why: one 64 K data segment -- the peak is every instruction in the translation
why: unit at once and a large module cannot be compiled at all.  The emit
why: boundary is the safe place to free: once genfunc() has run nothing points
why: into the list, because the fixups outcoh records name symbols and not
why: instructions.  Found by Michal Pleban compiling this tree natively

patch: optim-newlabel-nomem
file: n2/optim.c a757be22b37f962c07afa27c9fee313f83a3aad01c28615ac528b2889f1a7b7f
fix: `newlabel()' takes cfatal("out of space") on a failed malloc, in place of a NULL test whose `else' was missing
why: the donor initialises the record inside `if (lp != NULL)' and then returns
why: `lp' regardless, so an exhausted heap threads a NULL INS into the list.
why: On the C900 the dereference is a write through a 4-byte far pointer whose
why: segment is whatever the register happened to hold -- there is no mapped
why: page 0 to absorb it as there was on the donor's host -- and a 64 K heap is
why: one a real compile can actually exhaust.  cfatal is the pass's own
why: out-of-space exit (common/diag.c).  Found by Michal Pleban

patch: get-carriage-return
file: n0/get.c cc75dea4ec472417119bd3ab6b0e8e4653a48c3db679785be9133d75caff17cd
fix: both `\r' tests in get() -- skipped at a line start, and skipped between a backslash and its newline -- are unconditional, not `#if GEMDOS || MSDOS'
why: the donor guarded them as a DOS concern, but a carriage return is nothing
why: on every host: what the guard actually decides is whether a CR LF source
why: preprocesses correctly.  It does not.  The second test is the load-bearing
why: one -- with a CR between the backslash and the newline the splice is not
why: recognised, so a multi-line macro or string silently becomes something
why: else, with no diagnostic anywhere.  No file in this tree or in the kernel
why: and userland it compiles carries a CR today, so nothing here changes; it
why: is the C900 sources this compiler is aimed at next that are CR LF
why: throughout (upstream's own kernel tree), and a compiler that miscompiles
why: them quietly is worse than one that refuses them.  Found by Michal Pleban

patch: driver-eflag-preprocess-only
file: coh/cc.c 0364333a706756c33c00fa901778851151d7e01e1f6926754ed37bcf1ef5346f
fix: `if (Eflag) setvariant(VCPP);' moved out of `#if TEMPBUF' to the head of resolve()
why: TEMPBUF is defined nowhere in this tree -- it guards a COH386 malloc
why: efficiency hack -- so on the C900 driver that line never ran and -E did
why: not set VCPP.  resolve() then reaches `if (qpass == NONE)' with VCPP
why: clear and chooses qpass = LD, so `cc -E' preprocesses, compiles,
why: assembles and LINKS instead of stopping after the preprocessor.  Whether
why: a build buffers its temporary files has nothing to do with what -E means.
why: Found by Michal Pleban

patch: driver-vpic-option
file: coh/cc.c 0364333a706756c33c00fa901778851151d7e01e1f6926754ed37bcf1ef5346f
file: h/z8001/varmch.h
file: n1/z8001/mtree2.c
fix: a `#if Z8001' row for `-VPIC' in the option table, beside the other model
fix: flags, and its usage text under the same guard
why: the variant word is the ONLY channel from the driver to the passes, so a
why: machine-dependent variant bit that no driver row sets can be reached only
why: by spelling the hex word out by hand -- which is what the build scripts and
why: the gate would then have to do, putting the bit's number in three places
why: instead of one.  The bit itself (VPIC, VMBASE+9) and everything that reads
why: it are in the machine layer; this row is the four characters the MI table
why: needs to name it, guarded like the model rows the same table already
why: carries for this target.  On the C900 it selects the segmented machine's
why: only way to reference a shared library's data: an extern datum is addressed
why: through a 4-byte far pointer in the private data, which ld fills when the
why: symbol proves local and exec fills when it comes from a library.  No other
why: target defines VPIC, and with the bit clear the compiler emits what it
why: emitted before -- 121 userland programs and 215 libc objects byte-identical

patch: driver-implied-vpic
file: coh/cc.c 0364333a706756c33c00fa901778851151d7e01e1f6926754ed37bcf1ef5346f
fix: the driver reads each library on the link line, and where one is a shared
fix: library with an export table it links the shared start-off, drops the
fix: archive behind the library, and -- when the same command compiled the
fix: source -- sets VPIC itself; `-lNAME' with a `.' in NAME resolves to
fix: libNAME, as ld resolves it
why: the flag is not a taste: a client of such a library that lacks it cannot
why: be linked at all, and ld refuses it by name.  Who can know it is wanted is
why: the question, and the answer is the same as -fPIC's elsewhere -- a command
why: that both compiles and links knows what it is linking against before the
why: first pass runs, and a `-c' compile does not, so it is left alone and
why: static stays the default.  The library is recognised from the file, not
why: from its name: an l.out with LF_SLIB whose shared segment opens with the
why: `SL' export table of <shlib.h>.  A fixed-address library carries the flag
why: and no table, reaches its entries through an index, and wants none of
why: this, so it is not recognised and nothing is implied for it.  The probe
why: reads the header a byte at a time -- canonical shorts are low byte first,
why: the table is target memory, high byte first -- so the driver reads the
why: same file the same way whichever machine it was compiled for.  `-lc' and
why: every other undotted name take the archive they always took: the 122
why: userland programs and the libc objects come out byte-identical

patch: void-pointer
file: n0/ddecl.c 92064706183fdacc677581b46201d4fb56670dd2c5f2d2c6f08c301780997b5b
file: n0/gdecl.c 6c8fa941b1282cf3af5e63e23c4d9b3b9148ba68ffb5ee4668512035fe28a572
file: n0/expr.c 941c57539fd0f9b883130b5af6c8341e6cc17bbf4fa3554d642ec38d11e1fb86
file: h/cc0.h cb30ca61acf7c1552bb19b91579d0d55bae0f9a77b0ea858dd371edeef3558ca
fix: `void *' is `char *'.  A new `isvoidptr()' (n0/ddecl.c) answers whether a
fix: `void' declarator chain ends in a pointer -- the last dim is the one next
fix: to the base type -- and `declare()' and `cast()' take T_CHAR when it does.
fix: isvoidptr() also stamps that D_PTR's `d_bound', unread on a pointer, with
fix: the new `D_VOIDP' (h/cc0.h): `build()' refuses a dereference through a
fix: stamped pointer and `psize()' warns and scales arithmetic by 1 (n0/expr.c).
why: char * is this machine's typeless object pointer -- it is what the tree's
why: own headers spell NULL as, and what every K&R routine that hands back
why: storage returns -- so mapping `void *' onto it needs no conversion, no
why: representation and no cast in either direction, and the passes below cc0
why: never learn a new type.  The alternative, carrying T_VOID through a
why: pointer, would put a type with no size into the IR that cc1 and cc2 size
why: everything by.  It is a pointer that matters natively: a pointer here is a
why: 4-byte seg:offset, so the `((void *)0)' that outside headers define NULL
why: as must be a POINTER constant.  As an int it is pushed as two bytes and
why: the callee reads a garbage pointer and every argument behind it moves --
why: this project's most recurring bug class, and it costs the boot.  The stamp
why: is what `char *' cannot say by itself: a void pointer addresses no object,
why: so `*p' is an error and `p + 1' has no size to scale by.  Arithmetic warns
why: rather than errors and scales by 1, which is what the outside code that
why: wants `void *' at all already means by it; a dereference has no such
why: reading and is refused.  Nothing that compiled before changes: d_bound is
why: meaningless on a D_PTR and only a `void' declarator ever stamps one, so an
why: ordinary pointer takes the path it took, and `void' as a function return
why: or a plain object is refused exactly as before.  The mapping is Michal
why: Pleban's, from his own build of this compiler; the stamp and the two
why: refusals are ours.

patch: volatile-local
file: h/cc0.h cb30ca61acf7c1552bb19b91579d0d55bae0f9a77b0ea858dd371edeef3558ca
file: n0/gcandt.c fcb06fac89760c8c5ed36b65cf1ddee1f84e89582058cd715ba8e0b2f358a70d
file: n0/ddecl.c 92064706183fdacc677581b46201d4fb56670dd2c5f2d2c6f08c301780997b5b
file: n0/z8001/bind.c
fix: `gcandt()' returns `volatile' as `S_VOLAT' among the modifier flags it already returns for `readonly' and `alien', so `declare()' records it on the symbol. A qualifier on a parameter is not a redeclaration: the C_ARG placeholder the parameter list leaves behind carries no flags, and that arm already takes any combination. The machine layer then refuses both a register and the promotion offer to any local or parameter carrying the flag
why: the keyword was parsed and thrown away. While nothing held a local anywhere but its frame slot that cost nothing; now the callee-saved pool is handed out to locals the source never declared `register', and a volatile local would be read from a register that no longer matches the slot -- wrong exactly where the qualifier was asked for, and quietly. Nothing else in the compiler changes: a local without the qualifier is offered as before, and the flag is a new bit in `s_flag'

patch: volatile-memory
file: h/ops.h dcf446daf7e26941b4187f7234e2294c993448028a35ea42eed396bc5fe3c48b
file: h/cc0.h cb30ca61acf7c1552bb19b91579d0d55bae0f9a77b0ea858dd371edeef3558ca
file: h/cc1.h ad9eaee22669a51462c1027abede6574b6ae9b24206679793c6c4d9c2430e171
file: n0/expr.c 941c57539fd0f9b883130b5af6c8341e6cc17bbf4fa3554d642ec38d11e1fb86
file: n0/gdecl.c 6c8fa941b1282cf3af5e63e23c4d9b3b9148ba68ffb5ee4668512035fe28a572
file: n1/tree0.c 4e05e7114ccc76adcd5bd3d0f78a1f27cf369fd7368e8933eb672a21ad59ae7d
file: n1/out.c 516d8b7c37baf86c44cc8c0c83f3a5471095f5f3b843ac9afbb7ef84fe0a4ca4
file: n1/sel0.c 640bdfe6742a65726da0ae384eb1c52a02b8e401bfe91d7b011921fa80ccef53
file: h/z8001/mch.h
file: n1/z8001/gen1.c
file: n2/z8001/afield.c
file: n2/z8001/peep.c
fix: the qualifier rides the tree node from the identifier, the member or the pointer cast that declared it up to the indirection, and out on the tree type byte as `TVOL'; cc1 puts it on the operand it collects as `A_VOL', where the peephole reads it -- a volatile location names no stable value, so it is never tracked in a register, never forwarded, never substituted for, and a store and its reload are never collapsed. The qualifier on a struct member and in a pointer cast is accepted rather than diagnosed as a storage class
why: this is what a driver writes to say that reading a device register twice means two reads. Nothing carried the qualifier past the front end, so the back end forwarded the second read out of the register still holding the first and the accesses the source ordered did not happen -- wrong exactly where the qualifier was asked for. The member form and the device-register form `*(volatile char *)0xF000' had become hard errors once the front end began returning the flag, so both are taken here. An access without the qualifier is collected and optimized as before

patch: volatile-level
file: h/cc0.h cb30ca61acf7c1552bb19b91579d0d55bae0f9a77b0ea858dd371edeef3558ca
file: n0/cc0.c 5d09169339329ac3362d31f6764ed02dd97c992d876fe9080311e2e6e10cf83b
file: n0/locals.c 920492706fe61aa11625f3e830a1821d4de2c5ad63cd8bca7151d404fbd734b3
file: n0/gcandt.c fcb06fac89760c8c5ed36b65cf1ddee1f84e89582058cd715ba8e0b2f358a70d
file: n0/gdecl.c 6c8fa941b1282cf3af5e63e23c4d9b3b9148ba68ffb5ee4668512035fe28a572
file: n0/expr.c 941c57539fd0f9b883130b5af6c8341e6cc17bbf4fa3554d642ec38d11e1fb86
fix: the qualifier is placed at the declarator level it was written at. `volatile' after a `*' is read rather than skipped, and a typedef hands on the flags it was declared with. The object itself volatile is `S_VOLAT', as before; a pointer, array of pointers or function reaching volatile storage is the new `S_VTARG', which marks its identifier, member or cast `V_TARG' so only the indirection is volatile. `volqual()' sets both where each declarator is declared
why: `int * volatile p' and a `volatile' typedef lost the qualifier, so the object was promoted to a register and its accesses optimized -- wrong exactly where the qualifier was asked for. `volatile int *p' was the reverse: the flag sat on the symbol, so p itself was kept out of a register though only `*p' is volatile. A declaration with neither form compiles as before

patch: sinit-member-flags
file: n0/init.c f2516dcc7a10922f92b884e0f039919d72b5560c16fac7c6eb3ba30c0376579d
fix: `sinit()' sets the flags of its stack copy of the structure: a member initializer is volatile when the structure or the member is
why: the copy's `s_flag' was never set, and `bid()' reads it since identifiers carry volatile, so each member store took whatever the stack held -- randomly volatile, randomly a pointer to volatile.  The code generator keeps a volatile store in memory and never reuses its value, so the stack decided code; a volatile structure's initialization is now volatile as it should be

patch: itree-volatile
file: n3/itree.c 6425d15787cc09e743a695f2f8f90756f2b7d292b53c7a1ea877b52d6394f4c2
fix: the type byte's volatile bit is masked before the type is named, and printed as `volatile'
why: the front end marks a volatile access on the type byte; indexing the name table with the bit set printed garbage, and a volatile block type then skipped its size and read the rest of the file out of step

patch: ndbuf-machine-size
file: h/cc0.h cb30ca61acf7c1552bb19b91579d0d55bae0f9a77b0ea858dd371edeef3558ca
file: h/z8001/cc0mch.h
fix: the small-model NDBUF is `#ifndef' guarded so the machine layer can size the macro expansion buffer; h/z8001/cc0mch.h sets 4096
why: a machine sizing hook, not a defect: the donor already sizes NDBUF per target (8192 under `_I386'), and cc0mch.h, the one machine header cc0 reads, is included before the constant is defined, so no machine layer can set it without this guard. A target that does not define NDBUF keeps 1024. On the Z8001 an argument is held three times over while `substitute()' expands it -- as collected, as expanded by `argexpand()', and the expansion it pushes -- and every identifier in each copy carries a hide-set byte, so the donor's 1024 bytes stopped cc0 with "macro expansion buffer overflow" at an argument of about 300 characters: a test's `CHECK' of a dozen clauses over a few lines. The buffer cannot grow in place, since the dstack and the hide machinery hold pointers into it, so it is sized: 4096 takes an argument of about 1400 characters for 3 K more BSS in the native cc0, whose static data is about 15.5 K of its one 64 K data segment. The overflow stays the same fatal error with the buffer full. The constant reaches nothing but the bound tests, so no program that compiled before compiles differently; NDLEV is unchanged

patch: getid-pad-bound
file: n0/lex.c 5c59129078804743f2bfb5e47f7e4c94ca0f62846255b990c0ad92a89fdb7ec3
fix: `getid()' writes the second and third NUL after an identifier only while they fall inside id[]
why: a defect on every target, since id[] is NCSYMB bytes and NCSYMB is machine-independent: an identifier is stored up to NCSYMB-1 (255) characters and then followed by three NULs, so one of 254 or more characters wrote one or two bytes past id[] -- into `ifp', the input FILE pointer declared after it in n0/cc0.c, on the host and on the native C900 alike; the host cc0 then faulted on the next read. `newtoken()' copies the name plus one or two NULs to an even length, at most NCSYMB bytes, so every byte it copies is still written. An identifier under 254 characters gets the same three NULs as before, so no program that compiled before compiles differently

patch: ansi-prototypes
file: h/cc0.h cb30ca61acf7c1552bb19b91579d0d55bae0f9a77b0ea858dd371edeef3558ca
file: n0/gdecl.c 6c8fa941b1282cf3af5e63e23c4d9b3b9148ba68ffb5ee4668512035fe28a572
file: n0/ddecl.c 92064706183fdacc677581b46201d4fb56670dd2c5f2d2c6f08c301780997b5b
file: n0/expr.c 941c57539fd0f9b883130b5af6c8341e6cc17bbf4fa3554d642ec38d11e1fb86
file: n0/cc0.c 5d09169339329ac3362d31f6764ed02dd97c992d876fe9080311e2e6e10cf83b
fix: FEATURE ADDITION, not a defect fix (approved for the Workbench/intuition port). Function prototypes and ANSI definitions: `garglist()' and `cproto()' read a parameter type list wherever a function declarator appears -- declarations, definitions, typedefs, casts, sizeof, struct members, nested function-pointer parameters -- with named or abstract parameters, `(void)' and a trailing `...'. The list is kept as each parameter's promoted type, interned in a pool of 512-byte chunks, and a D_FUNC's d_bound holds its index (0 is `()', as before). An ANSI definition declares its parameters exactly as the K&R form does; the names of any other prototype are scoped to it (`dropargs()'). A call through a prototype converts each fixed argument as assignment would (int to long, a constant 0 to a 32-bit null, no integer/pointer mixing) and checks the count; `...' arguments keep the default promotions; under -VSVARG (record vsvarg-variant) an int in a `...' slot warns. `declare()' keeps a prototype across a `()' or K&R redeclaration and calls conflicting ones a redeclaration; `xfunc()' checks a K&R definition against an earlier prototype
why: additive: every new path is entered only by a parameter list that starts with a type, which the donor rejected as a syntax error, or by a D_FUNC whose d_bound is nonzero, which nothing but such a list creates; nothing reaches cc1 but ordinary trees, so an unprototyped program compiles byte-identical (shown over libc, libm, libc.1, the kernel and drivers, the userland and Exec900). No ABI change on the C900: a parameter is still passed as its promoted type (char/short/int a word, long and pointers two, float as double), so prototyped and K&R objects call each other and an ANSI definition compiles to exactly its K&R object. Bounded for the native cc0: a list costs 4 bytes plus one per parameter, once per distinct list, in at most 32 K of pool; 64 bytes of stack per list being read

patch: long-switch
file: h/ops.h dcf446daf7e26941b4187f7234e2294c993448028a35ea42eed396bc5fe3c48b
file: h/cc0.h cb30ca61acf7c1552bb19b91579d0d55bae0f9a77b0ea858dd371edeef3558ca
file: h/cc1.h ad9eaee22669a51462c1027abede6574b6ae9b24206679793c6c4d9c2430e171
file: n0/stat.c d10a870857a501013ec1e4cfa233277b7904b7b275a6f792ecd0eeec793c1743
file: n0/expr.c 941c57539fd0f9b883130b5af6c8341e6cc17bbf4fa3554d642ec38d11e1fb86
file: n1/cc1.c 0abdeced26fc7bb48bebfa0935d9d75288d70d35d0f6ab2659a799bad1feebe9
file: n1/code.c 747566dae0a5a81d9c31afcef4616d0376bf40f107ad3340dbf05e3637affc41
file: n3/cc3.c 5d60e32ad09cbe7eb361999939e592fb429a190511a5eae8d9950c1da2f4e46e
file: n1/z8001/cc1mch.h
file: n1/z8001/gen1.c
fix: FEATURE ADDITION, not a defect fix (approved for the Workbench/intuition port). `switch' accepts a long or unsigned long controlling expression. cc0 keeps it long, reads each case with `lconexpr()', which folds the constant through its casts and converts it to 32 bits by the C rules, checks duplicates on the converted values (SCASE holds an lval_t, SBLOCK says whether the switch is long), and ends it with the new LSBODY record: default, count, an unsigned flag, then value/label pairs as long/int. cc1 evaluates the value into `swreg()' of its type when the machine defines it, and hands the cases, sorted signed or unsigned, to the machine's `genlswitch()'; cc3 prints the record. On the Z8001 the value is in RR0: a dense range is rebased with SUBL, bounded with CPL and dispatched through the same self-relative table genswitch uses, indexed by R1, and only when the table is smaller than the compares; otherwise CPL compares in a binary search, down to runs of four. The peephole prescan copies the record
why: additive: the int path is untouched, and a long switch was an error before (`switch of non integer'), so no program that compiled compiles differently and the int switch code is the same byte for byte; SEXPR, SBODY and every other record keep their layout, LSBODY is a new number. On the native C900 long is two words and the full 32-bit range matters -- AmigaOS tags start at TAG_USER, 0x80000000 -- so the cases are kept and compared as 32-bit values, never truncated; a sparse set of tags never produces a table. The machine-dependent half (the register, the compare and table code) is in the Z8001 layer; the case buffer is malloc'd for the one switch, as the int one is

patch: line-comments
file: n0/get.c cc75dea4ec472417119bd3ab6b0e8e4653a48c3db679785be9133d75caff17cd
fix: FEATURE ADDITION, not a defect fix (approved for the Workbench/intuition port). C99 `//' comments: `get()' takes `//' outside a string or character constant, and outside a skipped conditional, as a comment to the end of the line, read as one space. A backslash-newline inside it continues it and is counted as a line; the newline itself is left in the input, so it ends the line, a #define's body or a directive as any newline does. The standalone preprocessor (VCPP), whose input may be assembler with `/' comments, is unchanged, and the donor's -VCPLUS path is kept as it was
why: additive: `//' outside a string was not C before -- the second `/' was always a syntax error, except in `a //* c */ b', which now reads as C99 says, and which no source in the comparison set contains: the library, kernel, driver, userland and Exec900 objects are byte-identical. No ABI or data change on the C900: the comment is consumed by the same character reader that consumes `/* */', with no buffer

patch: mixed-declarations
file: n0/locals.c 920492706fe61aa11625f3e830a1821d4de2c5ad63cd8bca7151d404fbd734b3
file: n0/stat.c d10a870857a501013ec1e4cfa233277b7904b7b275a6f792ecd0eeec793c1743
file: n0/cc0.c 5d09169339329ac3362d31f6764ed02dd97c992d876fe9080311e2e6e10cf83b
fix: FEATURE ADDITION, not a defect fix (approved for the Workbench/intuition port). Declarations after statements: a block's statements are read by `body()', which hands a statement that starts with a declaration specifier or a typedef name (not a label) to `locals()' and then `putautos()', exactly as the declarations at the head of the block are read, so a name is in scope from its declarator to the end of the block and its initializer runs where it stands. `for (T i = e; ...)' reads the declaration in a scope of its own, opened as a nested block's is and closed after the loop. A jump past a declaration is not checked, as C99 allows without VLAs
why: additive: before this, a declaration after a statement was a syntax error, so a block whose declarations are all at its head takes the same path as before and compiles byte-identical (libc, libm, libc.1, the kernel and drivers, the userland and Exec900). On the C900 a later declaration takes the next frame slots below the block's earlier ones, announced by the same AUTOS record a nested block's entry emits, and the block's exit restores the frame as before, so the frame is bounded by the deepest nesting as it was; no data is added to cc0

patch: vsvarg-variant
file: n0/expr.c 941c57539fd0f9b883130b5af6c8341e6cc17bbf4fa3554d642ec38d11e1fb86
file: coh/cc.c 0364333a706756c33c00fa901778851151d7e01e1f6926754ed37bcf1ef5346f
file: h/z8001/varmch.h
fix: FEATURE ADDITION, not a defect fix (requested for the Workbench/intuition port). The ansi-prototypes warning of an int in a `...' slot is read under its own variant, VSVARG, instead of VSRTVC, and `protoarg()' reads it only `#ifdef VSVARG'. h/z8001/varmch.h defines VSVARG as VMBASE+11; the driver's strict rows carry `-VSVARG' and its usage line under `#if Z8001', so `-VS' still sets it. Default off
why: VSRTVC is the donor's "risky things in truth contexts" and also warns of every pointer or long used as a condition (`if (p)', `while (l)'), so a port that asked for the `...' check through it got the truth-context warnings too: 61 of them in the Workbench utility.library port, none of them a defect. -VSRTVC is the donor's again. The machine-independent slots 0..33 are all in use, and VMBASE cannot move without renumbering every machine variant, so the bit takes the next free machine slot, as VPIC and VLNAME did; a target that does not define it has no such warning, as before the prototype work. The bit reaches only cc0's diagnostics, so the code is byte-identical with it set or clear (tests/proto.sh compares cc0's output)

patch: auto-struct-init
file: n0/init.c f2516dcc7a10922f92b884e0f039919d72b5560c16fac7c6eb3ba30c0376579d
file: n0/expr.c 941c57539fd0f9b883130b5af6c8341e6cc17bbf4fa3554d642ec38d11e1fb86
file: h/cc0.h cb30ca61acf7c1552bb19b91579d0d55bae0f9a77b0ea858dd371edeef3558ca
fix: FEATURE ADDITION, not a defect fix (approved for the Workbench/AROS port). An automatic structure or union may be initialized from a structure expression, as C89 allows: `init()' reads the initializer of an automatic structure that does not start with `{', `}' or a string as one expression, and when it is a structure of the same type (the same INFO) it emits the assignment `o = e' that `iassign()' builds for any automatic item, and returns. Otherwise the tree is kept in `itp' and the first scalar member takes it in place of reading its own expression -- `init()' at its leaf, `bfinit()' through the new `iconval()', the folding half of `iconexpr()' -- so the brace-less member list (`struct R o = 1, 2;') reads as before. A member of a brace list may be initialized the same way. A structure of another type is a type clash, as before
why: additive: a structure-valued initializer was a type clash before, in every position, and every other initializer reaches the same member code in the same order: the expression is read with the same `newtree()', `ininit' and LINE record the member read emitted, and nothing is output between the two points, so the brace lists, member lists and static initializers compile byte-identical (the library, kernel, driver, userland, Exec900 and Workbench objects). No ABI or data change on the C900: the initialization is the structure assignment cc1 already generates for `o = e', block move and all, and is object-identical to `struct R o; o = e;' (tests/structinit.sh); cc0 gains one static pointer

patch: init-cast-context
file: n1/mtree0.c 0841ad1b7c1785231e0154ad730a10d2bf12cc7dfdf585efde77d678351aaf38
file: n1/z8001/gen2.c
fix: `modtree()' modifies a CONVERT or CAST operand in the MINIT context when the conversion is in it, as it already does the operands of unary, address, indirection and binary operators; the donor passed MRVALUE there alone. Z8001 `iexpr()' then initializes a long from an address as the far pointer (ZGPTR, the i386 donor's `tt = PTR'), and rejects an address under a conversion to a word or byte as not constant
why: the MINIT context is what tells a machine layer that an address in an initializer is a link-time constant; losing it under a cast is wrong on every target whose machine layer reads it -- the i8086 LARGE model and the Z8001 pool a static address in an MRVALUE context, so `(unsigned long)a' in an initializer became the address of a literal-pool cell holding a, and `int i = (int)a' silently initialized i with that cell's offset -- and changes nothing on the i386, whose machine layer reads MINIT only at the root. On the C900 a far pointer and a long are the same two words (leaves.t shares the pair), so the datum is the pointer's relocated long, object-identical to `char *p = a' (tests/ptrlong.sh); AROS IPTR tables (keymaps, tag lists) need it. Only initializers with a cast over an address change: before they were `initializer too complex' or the pool cell above

patch: auto-array-size
file: n0/init.c f2516dcc7a10922f92b884e0f039919d72b5560c16fac7c6eb3ba30c0376579d
file: n0/expr.c 941c57539fd0f9b883130b5af6c8341e6cc17bbf4fa3554d642ec38d11e1fb86
file: n0/ddecl.c 92064706183fdacc677581b46201d4fb56670dd2c5f2d2c6f08c301780997b5b
file: n0/locals.c 920492706fe61aa11625f3e830a1821d4de2c5ad63cd8bca7151d404fbd734b3
file: h/cc0.h cb30ca61acf7c1552bb19b91579d0d55bae0f9a77b0ea858dd371edeef3558ca
fix: FEATURE ADDITION, not a defect fix (approved for the Workbench/AROS port). An automatic array declared without a bound takes it from its initializer, as C89 allows: `declare()' refuses such an array only without an initializer, and `locals()' hands it to `iflex()', which reads the initializer with `init()' as for a static array, keeping each item (the member's type, dimensions, offset and tree) and each LINE record that `expr()' would have written, in order, with the tree space kept until they are written; then it binds the array, writes its AUTOS record and writes the kept records as `iassign()' and `expr()' would have. A structure element of an automatic array goes through `init()', so it too may be initialized whole from a structure expression (record auto-struct-init)
why: additive: before this, an automatic array without a bound was an error (`cannot declare flexible automatic array'), and every other declaration takes the path it took; an element of a static array of structures still goes to `sinit()' directly, and an automatic one reaches `sinit()' through `init()' with the same braces, so the brace lists compile byte-identical (the library, kernel, driver, userland, Exec900 and Workbench objects). The kept records are the records the bounded array writes, in its order, so `long a[] = { 1, 2 };' is object-identical to `long a[2] = { 1, 2 };' (tests/autoarray.sh). On the C900 the array takes the frame slots its size needs, as before; the items are kept in one malloc'd list, about 16 bytes an item on the native compiler and reused by the next such array, and the trees of one initializer stay in the tree space until it is written

