fix: FEATURE ADDITION, not a defect fix (approved for the Workbench/AROS port). Language levels: h/cc0.h reads a level from a machine's variant bits VSTD0 and VSTD1 (h/z8001/varmch.h: VMBASE+12 and +13), 0 when a machine has none: 0 every addition, 1 K&R, 2 C89 (prototypes, structure initializers, sized automatic arrays, long switch), 3 C99 (C89, `//', declarations after statements and in a `for'). Each addition's entry point asks `stdok()' (n0/etc.c), which below its level is the error "<feature> is not K&R C (-VKR)" or "... C89 (-VC89)" and the addition then goes on as if allowed, so one use is one message. Two are refused the donor's way, as K&R code can hold the same text: under K&R, `protohere()' reads `f(T)' with T a typedef name as a parameter named T, and under K&R and C89 `get()' reads `//' as a division. The driver's -VKR, -VC89 and -VC99 set the two bits, the last one winning, and -VSTRICT turns on -VSBOOK under -VKR, else -VSVARG, all under `#if Z8001'; the man page and usage text describe them
why: additive: the default level, 0, takes every path as before, and on a machine without the bits the level is a constant 0, so every object is byte-identical (the library, kernel, driver, userland, Exec900 and Workbench objects). At any level K&R code takes the same paths, since only an addition asks for its level (tests/stdlevel.sh compares cc0's output for every libc source at all four levels), so -VKR compiles K&R code as the donor did, `f(T)' included. The machine-independent slots are full and VMAXIM cannot grow without lengthening every variant string, so the level takes the last two free machine slots and -VSTRICT takes none: an addition above the level is always an error, and -VSTRICT turns on the existing strict messages that check the level. No ABI or data change on the C900; cc0 gains one function

