fix: FEATURE ADDITION, not a defect fix (requested for the Workbench/AROS port). -VSTRICT is an error mode: VSTRICT (h/z8001/varmch.h, VMBASE+14, bit 48) makes `cstrict()' (common/diag.c, and the host build's port of it) count every strict message as an error, printed even under VNOWARN; the driver's -VSTRICT sets it besides the level's own check (-VSBOOK under -VKR, else -VSVARG). A feature above the language level is an error with or without it. For the bit, VMAXIM grows from 48 to 56, a seventh variant byte; `makvariant()' leaves that byte off when it is zero (VMWRIT, 48 bits always written), and `getvariant()' already stops at the end of the string, so a string is twelve hex digits as before unless a bit of the new byte is set
why: additive: VMAXIM sizes only the VARIANT array and the driver's string buffers, every slot keeps its number, every twelve-digit string written by hand, a Makefile or ccz reads as before (its seventh byte zero), and the driver writes the same twelve digits for every command without -VSTRICT, so every object is byte-identical (the library, kernel, driver, userland, Exec900 and Workbench objects). The machine-independent slots 0..33 and the machine slots 34..47 were full. On the C900 each pass's variant array grows by one byte; with VSTRICT clear `cstrict()' is the donor's
